263	// How an `auto-merge` member's PR actually lands, given whether this repo has any
264	// pull_request-triggered workflow at all.
265	//
266	// GitHub's auto-merge is a QUEUE FOR CHECKS. On a repo with no PR CI there is
267	// nothing to queue behind: the mutation is rejected ("Pull request is in clean
268	// status"), and since the failure was swallowed the PR sat open forever — the
269	// stamp never advanced, the next cycle reused the same PR, and a repo that asked
270	// for `auto-merge` got no merge at all. Seven of twelve consumers are in exactly
271	// that shape (no `pull_request` trigger anywhere in .github/workflows).
272	//
273	// So: no CI to wait for → MERGE IT, which is what `auto-merge` meant all along.
274	// CI present → arm, and let the checks gate it as designed.
275	//
276	// `hasPrCi` comes from ciDispatchPlan over the branch's own workflow files —
277	// `dispatch` (startable) plus `missing` (PR-triggered but not dispatchable). Both
278	// empty means no workflow anywhere runs on a pull_request, so no check will ever
279	// appear. That is a fact about the tree, not a race against checks registering.
280	export function deliveryAction({ delivery, hasPrCi }) {
281	  if (delivery !== 'auto-merge') return 'none';
282	  return hasPrCi ? 'arm' : 'merge';
283	}
